      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The vanishing act of sublimation ink can be quite frustrating. It's not uncommon for polyester fabrics to be a bit finicky, but I've seen some cases where the ink just refuses to cooperate. You know, like a mischievous goblin playing tricks on the printer. \n\nLet's start with the basics. Have you checked the heat press temperature and pressure settings? If it's too low, the ink might not be able to sublimate properly, causing it to \"disappear\" on the fabric. It's a bit like a goblin hiding behind a curtain, waiting for the right moment to strike. \n\nAlso, ensure that the ink is compatible with the polyester fabric. Some inks might not be able to handle the heat and pressure, resulting in a \"poof\" of color. You might need to try a different ink or adjust the heat press settings to find the sweet spot. \n\nAnother possibility is that the fabric is not properly pre-treated or coated with a sublimation-friendly finish. It's like a goblin trying to hide in a patch of thorns – the ink just can't penetrate the fabric properly. \n\nLastly, check the paper quality and make sure it's not too thin or fl",
