      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sorry about that, I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's get back to the technical stuff.\n\nYou're on the right track with the fabric's chemical structure. Polyester fibers have a unique molecular structure that can affect the sublimation process. The chemical structure of polyester is based on a repeating unit of polyethylene terephthalate (PET), which has a crystalline structure that can influence the dye's ability to penetrate and sublimate.\n\nWhen the ink comes into contact with the fabric, the dye molecules must penetrate the crystalline structure of the PET fibers. If the fibers are not properly aligned or if there are imperfections in the fabric's molecular structure, the dye molecules may not be able to penetrate evenly, resulting in uneven or incomplete sublimation.\n\nThis phenomenon is often referred to as \"fiber orientation\" or \"fiber alignment.\" If the fibers are not properly aligned, the dye molecules may not be able to migrate to the surface of the fabric, resulting in a loss of color or a \"vanishing\" effect.\n\nIn terms of chemical structure, the PET fibers have a hydrophobic (water-repelling) surface, which can make it difficult for the dye molecules to penetrate. The dye",
